+# Deploying the tip jar frontend to IPFS
+
+The site ships as a **static Next.js export** pinned on IPFS. There is no server, so everything the
+app needs — the target chain, the contract address, the Open Graph origin — is baked into the
+bundle at build time and cannot be changed without producing a new CID.
+
+Nothing in this document has been run against a live network or uploaded anywhere. The build
+configuration below is in place and the static export has been produced and checked locally
+(see [Verify before uploading](#3-verify-before-uploading)).
+
+## What is already configured
+
+`packages/nextjs/next.config.ts`, when `NEXT_PUBLIC_IPFS_BUILD=true`:
+
+- `output: "export"` — static HTML/JS, no server
+- `trailingSlash: true` — every route becomes `<route>/index.html`, which is what a gateway can serve
+- `images: { unoptimized: true }` — the image optimizer needs a server
+- **fails the build** if the target network is a local chain (31337/1337), which would ship a bundle
+  pointing at `http://127.0.0.1:8545`
+- **fails the build** if `NEXT_PUBLIC_PRODUCTION_URL` is unset, which would bake
+  `http://localhost:3000` into the Open Graph and Twitter tags
+
+`packages/nextjs/package.json` passes `NODE_OPTIONS=--no-experimental-webstorage` to every Next
+command. On Node 25 the built-in `localStorage` global exists without the standard Web Storage
+methods unless a backing file is configured; wagmi/RainbowKit detect the global and call
+`getItem()` during prerender, which crashes the export with
+`TypeError: localStorage.getItem is not a function`. The flag removes the global so those libraries
+take their no-storage path. It is set at the process level so build workers inherit it.
+
+## 1. Deploy the contract to Base
+
+The frontend cannot target Base until the jar exists there. Do this first.
+
+```bash
+yarn generate                                   # or: yarn account:import
+yarn account                                    # confirm the deployer has ETH on Base
+yarn deploy --file DeployTipJar.s.sol --network base
+```
+
+This writes an `8453` entry into `packages/nextjs/contracts/deployedContracts.ts`. Verify the
+contract:
+
+```bash
+yarn verify --network base
+```
+
+Record the deployed address — the frontend build is only valid for that address.
+
+## 2. Point the frontend at Base and build
+
+In `packages/nextjs/scaffold.config.ts`, change the target network:
+
+```diff
+- targetNetworks: [chains.foundry],
++ targetNetworks: [chains.base],
+```
+
+Do this **after** step 1. Scaffold-ETH derives `ContractName` from the contracts declared for the
+configured chains, so switching first — before `deployedContracts.ts` has an `8453` entry — makes
+`TipJar` unresolvable and fails type checking.
+
+Then build, setting the origin the site will actually be served from:
+
+```bash
+cd packages/nextjs
+NEXT_PUBLIC_PRODUCTION_URL="https://tipjar.eth.limo" yarn build:ipfs
+```
+
+`build:ipfs` removes `.next` and `out` first, so no stale artifact survives into the upload.
+
+If you use a WalletConnect project id or your own Alchemy key, pass them too — they are inlined at
+build time and there is no runtime environment to read them from later:
+
+```bash
+NEXT_PUBLIC_PRODUCTION_URL="https://tipjar.eth.limo" \
+NEXT_PUBLIC_WALLET_CONNECT_PROJECT_ID="..." \
+NEXT_PUBLIC_ALCHEMY_API_KEY="..." \
+  yarn build:ipfs
+```
+
+## 3. Verify before uploading
+
+A bad bundle is cheap to fix now and expensive to fix after it has a CID.
+
+```bash
+cd packages/nextjs
+
+# Every route emitted its own directory with an index.html
+find out -name index.html | sort
+
+# The expected page is actually in the build
+grep -c "Leave a tip" out/index.html            # expect 1
+
+# Open Graph tags carry the production origin, not localhost
+grep -o '<meta property="og:[^>]*>' out/index.html
+grep -o '<meta name="twitter:image"[^>]*>' out/index.html
+
+# The bundle targets Base (8453), not the local fork (31337)
+grep -rlq "127.0.0.1:8545" out/ && echo "LOCAL RPC LEAKED INTO BUILD" || echo "no local RPC in build"
+```
+
+Then serve `out/` at a URL root and click through it — this is the closest local approximation of a
+gateway:
+
+```bash
+npx serve out            # or: python3 -m http.server -d out 8899
+```
+
+## 4. Upload
+
+The bundled helper builds and uploads in one step (it re-runs `build:ipfs`, so pass the same env):
+
+```bash
+cd packages/nextjs
+NEXT_PUBLIC_PRODUCTION_URL="https://tipjar.eth.limo" yarn ipfs
+```
+
+Or upload the already-verified `out/` directory with your own pinning setup:
+
+```bash
+# Kubo, local node
+ipfs add --recursive --cid-version 1 --quieter packages/nextjs/out
+
+# Pinata
+npx pinata-cli upload packages/nextjs/out
+```
+
+Use `--cid-version 1`. CIDv1 is base32 and case-insensitive, which is what subdomain gateways and
+ENS content hashes need. Convert an existing v0 CID with `ipfs cid base32 <cid>`.
+
+## 5. Verify after uploading
+
+**Record the CID.** If it is unchanged from the previous release, the uploaded bytes are identical —
+check the build output and the upload target before blaming gateway caching.
+
+```bash
+CID=<cid from step 4>
+```
+
+Check the root and at least one non-home route. A working root does not prove the exported route
+directories resolve:
+
+```bash
+curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" -L "https://$CID.ipfs.dweb.link/"
+curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" -L "https://$CID.ipfs.dweb.link/debug/"      # expect 200, not 404
+curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" -L "https://$CID.ipfs.dweb.link/blockexplorer/"
+```
+
+Confirm the Open Graph image and URL resolve to the production origin, and that the image itself is
+reachable:
+
+```bash
+curl -s -L "https://$CID.ipfs.dweb.link/" | grep -o '<meta property="og:[^>]*>'
+curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" -L "https://tipjar.eth.limo/thumbnail.jpg"
+```
+
+Finally load the site in a browser, connect a wallet on Base, and confirm the feed and the jar
+totals populate from the Base contract — an HTTP 200 only proves the HTML shipped, not that the
+frontend is pointed at the right chain and address.
+
+### Use a subdomain gateway, not a path gateway
+
+Verify on `https://<cid>.ipfs.dweb.link/` (subdomain) or through ENS/DNSLink — **not**
+`https://<gateway>/ipfs/<cid>/` (path).
+
+Next.js emits absolute asset paths (`/_next/static/chunks/...`). Under a path gateway the browser
+resolves those against the gateway root, not against `/ipfs/<cid>/`, so the HTML returns 200 while
+every script 404s and the page renders blank. Verified locally: serving `out/` at `/ipfs/<cid>/`
+returns 200 for the HTML and 404 for `/_next/static/chunks/*.js`. Serving it at a URL root works.
+
+There is no build-time fix — `assetPrefix: "./"` would break nested routes instead — so the site must
+be served from an origin root.
+
+## 6. ENS
+
+Update the content hash **only after** the deployed CID has been reviewed and approved in a browser.
+
+```bash
+# Set the content hash on the ENS name (via app.ens.domains or your own tooling), then confirm:
+cast resolve-name tipjar.eth --rpc-url mainnet
+curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" -L "https://tipjar.eth.limo/"
+curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" -L "https://tipjar.eth.limo/debug/"
+```
+
+Confirm both the resolver record and a public gateway serve the CID you intended, not the previous
+one. Gateways cache DNSLink and ENS records; if the old site is still served, re-check the resolver
+before rebuilding.
+
+## Release checklist
+
+- [ ] `TipJar` deployed and verified on Base; address recorded
+- [ ] `scaffold.config.ts` targets `chains.base`
+- [ ] `NEXT_PUBLIC_PRODUCTION_URL` set to the origin the site will be served from
+- [ ] `out/` has one directory per route; no `127.0.0.1:8545` in the bundle; OG tags use the production origin
+- [ ] CID recorded and different from the previous release
+- [ ] Root **and** a non-home route return 200 through a subdomain gateway
+- [ ] Page loads in a browser and reads live data from the Base contract
+- [ ] ENS content hash updated last, then re-verified
